here it is. This proves the OS-update checksum path completely.
At 0x1cc66, the caller loads:
r1 = 0x236f8
r0 = 0x21038
bl  0x1b680

We already know from the string table:
file offset 0x11038 -> /home/idec/usr/tmp/os_update.znx
file offset 0x136f8 -> /home/idec/usr/tmp/os_update.znx.md5

and this ELF maps those strings with the +0x10000 virtual-address offset:
0x11038 -> VA 0x21038
0x136f8 -> VA 0x236f8

So the call at 0x1cc78 is unequivocally:
verify_md5(
    "/home/idec/usr/tmp/os_update.znx",
    "/home/idec/usr/tmp/os_update.znx.md5"
);

The disassembly shows exactly those two addresses being placed in r0 and r1 immediately before the call to the MD5 helper.    Pasted text
And we have already reconstructed 0x1b680: it stats and opens the first argument, mmaps the entire file, feeds its full size to MD5_Update, formats all 16 digest bytes using %02x, reads 17 bytes from the second argument, and compares the first 16 characters with strncmp.    Pasted text
So we can now state this as fact:
os_update.znx
os_update.znx.md5
       |
       v
   idec_boot.BIN
       |
       +-- MD5 entire ZNX
       +-- compare checksum
       |
       +-- ZNX processing/extraction
       |
       +-- /home/idec/usr/tmp/os_update.tar.xz
       |
       +-- XZ CRC64
       |
       +-- extract to /mnt/rootfsA
       +-- install/write boot content

And look what happens immediately afterward:
1cc78  call verify_md5
1cc7c  save result
1cc7e  if result == 0 -> 1cc90

1cc90  r0 = 0x236f8
1cc98  remove(...)

So on a successful checksum, it deletes:
/home/idec/usr/tmp/os_update.znx.md5



